<Base title="Le Donjon, grandeur nature" current="/en/the-donjon/" lang="en" alt="/le-donjon/">
  <p class="eyebrow">Before L'AgorA</p>
  <h1>Le Donjon, grandeur nature!</h1>
  <p class="lede">"From the intimate donjon of the 5505… to the donjon, life-size!" The private club of artists and musicians that preceded L'AgorA, from Montreal to Saint-Fortunat, 2008–2011.</p>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>The beginnings: Amandine's basement</h2>
    <p>The Donjon is first of all the creation of Amandine, alias Arte Mis. In 2008, the idea and the initiative are entirely hers: in her basement at 5505 Saint-Urbain Street, in Montreal, she puts up the red drapes, the hammocks, the sofas, the Buddha and the turntables of "the intimate donjon". Christian joins her a year later, in 2009. When the parties get too loud for the neighbours and the roommates, they rent a space together on Park Avenue: the Donjon on Parc, from January to March 2010, three intense months that end because the place has become too popular. Back to the 5505 in April, then, tired of being constantly on high alert, they accept the offer of a place in Saint-Fortunat, where the Donjon becomes "life-size" for a year, before becoming L'AgorA after Amandine's departure.</p>

    <h2>A private club of 400 members</h2>
    <p>The ledonjon.ca website, put online in November 2010 "after several months of inactivity and the establishment's move to the country", presented the Donjon this way: a private club, counting to date more than 400 members, "where one must ask the administrators for a new membership". To become a member, all you had to do was show up at an event, pay the admission and ask to join. "There are several types of members, probationary, regular and privileged. It goes without saying that your participation will define your status within this organization where art is alive."</p>
    <blockquote><p>A private space in nature… where everything is placed at the disposal of the subject, so that they may make of themselves the subject of the work in becoming.</p><cite>Home page of ledonjon.ca, December 2010</cite></blockquote>
    <p>On the Donjon's Facebook group, the description said the same thing differently: "A space of free and avant-garde creativity… Organized and improvised parties, happenings and workshops."</p>
    <p>In February 2011, the site is rebuilt: "welcome to our new site". The Donjon is "now in the middle of nature, located on a 100-acre land in St-Fortunat", "2 h 15 from Montreal, 1 h 15 from Quebec City", and "offers outdoor and indoor camping spaces in the summer season as well as in winter. Furnished spaces for your events are available by reservation." The Contacts page introduces the two administrators. Christian "has been producing events for more than 10 years", "expert computer technician and Apple product repairman, expert in stage technique", who "offers solutions adapted to your specific needs: he is an expert in finding new marginal solutions". Amandine "has been promoting artists for more than 10 years; she creates events where visual and musical arts combine and harmonize for the pure pleasure of the senses; she is also a decorator, all for the pleasure of perfecting your environment". Hers, the camping and site reservations; his, the technical side, "management of your event from A to Z".</p>

    <h2>The website, from 2010 to 2019</h2>
    <p>The ledonjon.ca website had several lives, which the Wayback Machine kept in pieces. On March 7, 2010, before the move even, a single page, "Le Donjon, espace créatif", promises: "Coming soon on the web!! Stay tuned!" In November 2010, the complete site, preserved on the hard drive. In February 2011, the redesign, with the Contacts page, the Sundari programme, the shuttle. On September 28, 2012, one last update changes everything, down to the name:</p>
    <blockquote><p>L'agora, de St-Fortunat (formerly le Donjon grandeur nature!) — "Techno-bohemian campground". Multidisciplinary artistic collective, multi-purpose space. Four-season camping: outdoor camping spaces (fields, woods) and indoor ones (community hall called the "bunker", barn, yurt, treehouse).</p><cite>Home page of ledonjon.ca, September 2012</cite></blockquote>
    <p>In February 2013, a "Water Snake" page; from June 4, 2013 on, the address leads straight to the Tatanka festival's page, the only one whose pictures survived. From 2016 to 2019, the domain shows nothing but the host's default page. In 2026, ledonjon.ca is free. The recovered pages are in the <a href="/archives/ledonjon-ca-wayback/">archives</a>.</p>

    <h2>The Lune Noire</h2>
    <p>"Every month… always around the new moons… a gathering of artists, visual at first, creativity workshops, painting, crafts, thrift… then musical, jam sessions. Open to all, artists and creators!" The concept of the Lune Noire nights began in 2002 in a loft in Old Montreal, "where every month artist and musician friends met to jam and dance". You came with your instruments, "your voices, your legs, your hips and your good mood", and something to eat for the potluck. "For a quality jam, let's listen to one another."</p>

    <h2>The member artists</h2>
    <p>The site's "The artists" page thanked those who had come to play at the 5505 and "for the friends of the donjon": Xnorophis, from Edinburgh, "big thanks for your support throughout the donjon's evolution"; DJ Zen, from Inverness, founder of Altar Records, who came to launch his CD <em>Earth</em> on March 12, 2010; D♪ane LOngstOcking, "who knows very well where to take us at the right moment"; Psyberth, "a pearl with a contagious smile"; Cosmic Orgasm, who came "with all their gear to practise in front of the friends of the donjon"; LutinDesBois, from Paris, "we assure you, this guy is an angel"; DJ Pranapapa, from Quebec City, "for the psychill-techno-world". And the decorators: Arte Mis, hlystän Lùnä and her geodesic domes, Waking-Dream.</p>
    <p>The New Year's Eve Party 2011 lined up "our favourite DJs": Alakazoo, Bendala, Geetee, Jawstyle, NeuroVyrus, PranaPapa, SabiNonStop, View-zik, Xnorophis live, Zen, Zven.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 class="eyebrow" style="margin-top:2rem">Amandine's Donjon at the 5505, Montreal, 2008–2010</h2>
